  <li><p>Graph both functions on the grid.</p>
      <image source="act-5-3-6.png" width="50%"><description>grid [-5,5]x[-2,6]</description></image></li>

  <li><p>State the intervals on which each graph is increasing. Write a few sentences comparing the two graphs.</p></li>
</ol></p>
</activity>

<subsection><title>Checkpoint</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>In Activity 1a, which is larger, <m>(\frac{1}{2})^2</m> or <m>(\frac{1}{2})^3</m>? How is this reflected in the graphs?</p></li>     
    <li><p>In Activity 1c, why are there no <m>y</m>-values on the graph for negative <m>x</m>-values?</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 2b, explain why the points <m>(1,1)</m> and <m>(-1,-1)</m> are helpful in sketching the graph of <m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>In Activity 3, what are the slopes of the two branches of the graph of <m>y=\abs{x}</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></subsection>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Questions for Writing or Discussion</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Describe the differences between the graphs of <m>f(x)=x^2</m> and <m>g(x)=x^3</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>Describe how we can use a vertical asymptote to help us sketch a graph.</p></li>
    <li><p>Compare the graphs of <m>\dfrac{1}{x}</m> and <m>\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m> for <m>0 \le x \le 1</m>.</p></li>
    <li><p>What connections do you see between the graphs of <m>y=x^3</m> and <m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m>?</p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

<assemblage component="instructor"><title>Concept Questions</title>
<p><ol>
    <li><p>Which of the eight basic functions are undefined at <m>x=0</m>?  (Choose all that apply.)</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=x</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^3</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\abs{x}</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which of the eight basic functions is undefined for negative <m>x</m>? (Choose all that apply.)</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=x</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^3</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\abs{x}</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>Which of the eight basic functions is always positive?(Choose all that apply.)</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>y=x</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^2</m></li>
        <li><m>y=x^3</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\sqrt[3]{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\dfrac{1}{x^2}</m></li>
        <li><m>y=\abs{x}</m></li>
        </ol></p></li>
    <li><p>If <m>\abs{x} = -x</m>, what can you say about <m>x</m>?</p>
    <p><ol marker="a.">
        <li><m>x</m> must be zero.</li>
        <li><m>x</m> must be negative.</li>
        <li><m>x</m> must be zero or negative.</li>
        <li>This cannot happen for any value of <m>x</m>.</li>
    </ol></p></li>
</ol></p></assemblage>

</section>